<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basel-Stadt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Basel-City</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basel-Stadt is a canton of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> made up of the city of Basel and the two small communes of Riehen and Bettingen, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> where Switzerland meets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It was created in 1833 when the rural districts split off as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basel-landschaft">Basel-Landschaft</a>. It is the smallest Swiss canton by area and a centre of the chemical and pharmaceutical industries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basel-stadt.jpg" alt="Basel-Stadt" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basel-Stadt_in_Switzerland_(special_marker).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Basel-Stadt, or Basel-City, is one of the 26 cantons of the Swiss Confederation, composed of three municipalities, Basel, Riehen and Bettingen, with Basel as its capital. It is traditionally a half-canton, the other half being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basel-landschaft">Basel-Landschaft</a>. It is the smallest canton by area, just 37 square kilometres, and one of the northernmost and lowest, lying on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Basel stands at the Rhine knee, where the river turns from a westerly to a northerly flow, marking the change from the High Rhine to the Upper Rhine. The territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> meet in the middle of the Rhine, about 150 metres north of the Dreiländereck monument.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basel-landschaft">Basel-Landschaft</a>, Basel-Stadt was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of Basel, which joined the Old Swiss Confederacy in 1501. After political quarrels and armed conflict, partly over the rights of the population of the agricultural areas, the canton was divided and Basel-Landschaft separated from the city on 26 August 1833. A movement for reunification gained momentum after 1900, when many parts of Basel-Landschaft became industrialised, but the people of Basel-Landschaft voted against reunion in 1969 and again in September 2014.</p><p>The present constitution of the canton dates from 1889. In 1966 Basel-Stadt became the first German-speaking canton to allow women to vote, five years before the right was extended to all Swiss women in 1971.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The chemical and pharmaceutical industries are the most significant industries in the canton, and Basel is the worldwide seat of the Bank for International Settlements. The port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> offers landlocked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> its only direct connection to the sea, and the airport of Basel-Mulhouse lies 4 kilometres inside French territory. The Carnival of Basel, whose central tradition, the Morgestraich, begins at 4 a.m. and is followed by 72 hours of festivities, and the Autumn Fair, the biggest in Switzerland, are major events. The canton is known for two biscuits, the Basler Läckerli and the Basler Brunsli.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Basel-Stadt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Restored in 1803, the canton split in 1833 after civil conflict into <b>Basel-Stadt</b> and Basel-Landschaft, which remain separate half-cantons.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-basel">Canton of Basel</a></li></ul></section>
